It seems to me that there's an argument that your shirt should be the
first
thing you enchant, if you're not playing a 'caster who'll need a Helm
of
Brilliance, or someone who'll need Gauntlets of Dexterity.* Namely,
enchantments are just as effective no matter what armor they're on,
and you're never going to want to change your shirt (unless you find
a shirt that's already enchanted, and that's highly unlikely, though I
did
find a +1 shirt in one game). Also it doesn't matter if your shirt
gets
burnt (if they even burn), since it already has 0 natural AC.

The only catch is that you can lose your shirt to a polytrap (or to a
destroy armor spell, but that could affect any piece of armor you
enchant). Given that, you might want to enchant leather gloves first,
if you're not going to want Gauntlets of Power or Dexterity (see
below); but a Valk probably will want Power, so it doesn't apply.

Also, given the number of leather gloves that get dropped in the
game, it seems more likely to me that you'll find some already
enchanted (unless soldiers and the like never have enchanted
gloves); also gloves can get rotted which might make you want
to switch to a new pair. That is, someone with +0 rotted gloves
and +2 shirt can get a point of AC by switching to +0 unrotted
gloves that they find; someone with +2 rotted gloves and +0
shirt will find it harder to get that AC back.

Whether this makes a shirt worth the money at an early point
in the game is another question. I don't know -- I'm actually a
terrible procrastinator as far as enchanting my armor goes.
Anyway, it's just a thought, not one I've tested extensively.

*Actually, more experienced hackers can answer me this:
Do you ever really need gauntlets of dexterity? By the time
most of my characters would be in position to drop a lot of
enchantments on GoDex, they never miss anyway. Is
there any other big advantage to them?


